30 min
MODULE 2.1
Global work culture decoded: six behaviours that get you kept
Ownership over instruction
You own the outcome, not the task list. If the 
reconciliation needs a source document, you chase it — 
you do not wait to be told.
Documentation is the culture
If it is not written down, it did not happen. Meeting 
notes, decisions, and handovers live in shared docs, not 
in your head.
Honest status, early
'On track', 'at risk', or 'blocked' — said the moment you 
know. Bad news early is professional; bad news late is a 
firing offence.
Feedback is direct and normal
A UK manager saying 'this isn't quite right' is coaching, 
not conflict. Ask what good looks like, fix it, move on.
Deadlines mean the deadline
'Friday' means before their end of day on Friday, in their 
time zone. If it will slip, renegotiate on Wednesday.
Confidentiality and boundaries
Client data stays in client systems. Working hours are 
respected both ways — you are not expected to answer 
at 11pm, and you should not expect it either.
Get Global Workforce Ready  ·  NYSC Corps Member Pathway11

40 min
MODULE 2.2
Time zones and the remote day: WAT against the world
Lagos runs on West Africa Time (UTC+1) all year. Your clients move their clocks twice a year — you do not. Learn the two numbers for every client zone.
Client locationNorthern winter (Nov–Mar)Northern summer (Mar–Oct)Quick conversionRealistic overlap
London / UKGMT (UTC+0) · Lagos −1hBST (UTC+1) · same as LagosTheir 9:00 = your 10:00 (winter) / 9:00 (summer)Full day
New York / US EastEST (UTC−5) · Lagos −6hEDT (UTC−4) · Lagos −5hTheir 9:00 = your 15:00 (winter) / 14:00 (summer)Your 14:00–19:00
Toronto / Canada EastEST (UTC−5) · Lagos −6hEDT (UTC−4) · Lagos −5hTheir 9:00 = your 15:00 / 14:00Your 14:00–19:00
Dubai / UAEGST (UTC+4) · Lagos +3hNo changeTheir 9:00 = your 6:00Your 8:00–15:00
Sydney / Australia EastAEST (UTC+10) · Lagos +9hAEDT (UTC+11) · Lagos +10hTheir 9:00 = your midnight / 23:00
Your 8:00–10:00 (their late 
afternoon)
•Set your calendar to show two time zones (yours + client's). Never convert in your head on a call.
•Write every deadline with a zone: 'Thursday 5pm BST'. Never bare 'Thursday'.
•Australia's DST runs Oct–Apr — opposite to the north. Check the date before you promise a time.
•Protect the overlap window for live work; use the non-overlap hours for deep work and hand-offs.
Get Global Workforce Ready  ·  NYSC Corps Member Pathway12

30 min
MODULE 2.3
Time management that survives NEPA: the reliable remote day
A model day for a UK client (BST)
08:30
Power & connection check; open the day's task list; charge 
everything.
09:00
Stand-up update posted (see format). Reply to overnight messages.
09:30–12:30
Deep-work block: the highest-value deliverable, notifications off.
12:30–13:30
Lunch. Step away from the screen.
13:30–16:00
Overlap block: calls, reviews, questions the client needs live.
16:00–17:00
Close: end-of-day note, tomorrow's list, files saved to shared drive.
THE 3-LINE STAND-UP (POST DAILY, 9:00 CLIENT TIME)
Yesterday: completed Q3 bank reconciliation (3 accounts); variance report 
sent.Today: AP ageing analysis; draft supplier statements by 16:00 BST.Blockers: 
need read access to the Xero contacts module — requested from Tom.
CONTINGENCY: THE PLAN-B CHECKLIST
•Power: inverter or power bank rated for 4+ hours of laptop use; know your nearest 
co-working space.
•Internet: two providers (fibre or 4G/5G router + a phone hotspot on a different 
network).
•Device: laptop battery healthy; files synced to cloud so any device can continue.
•Communication: tell the client within 10 minutes of any outage, with a return time.
Get Global Workforce Ready  ·  NYSC Corps Member Pathway13

20 min
MODULE 2.4 · WORKSHOP
Plan a two-time-zone week
The brief
You are a KYC analyst placed with a London compliance team (BST) whose head of operations sits in 
Toronto (EDT). You have:
•A weekly team stand-up London wants at their 9:30.
•A one-to-one Toronto wants at their 10:00 on Wednesdays.
•A report due 'Friday end of day' to Toronto.
•A personal commitment every Tuesday 17:00–19:00 Lagos.Produce a one-page weekly plan in 
Lagos time: fixed meetings, two deep-work blocks a day, the overlap window, and the latest 
safe delivery time for the Friday report. State one assumption you had to make.
MARKING GUIDE
•Both meetings converted correctly to Lagos time.
•Friday delivery set before 23:00 Lagos (Toronto 18:00 
EDT) — with buffer.
•Deep-work blocks placed outside the overlap window.
•Tuesday commitment protected and communicated.
•Assumption stated explicitly (e.g. which DST period).
Homework: set up two-zone display on your own calendar and 
screenshot it.
